WEBVTT

00:00:00.878 --> 00:00:05.177
Every website can be split up into two
parts, the front end and the back end.

00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:09.494
The front end is all the visual stuff
you see on the webpage, the images, the

00:00:09.494 --> 00:00:14.019
text, the buttons, and the back end is
what saves and manages your data, for

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.955
example, your Amazon order history.

00:00:16.519 --> 00:00:19.613
In this video, I'm gonna explain all
the technologies that are used in the

00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:23.780
front end of a web application, and in
another video, we'll explore the back end.

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:24.942
Let's get started

00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:30.174
We're gonna start with a blank
webpage, and the first technology

00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:32.341
we're gonna look at is HTML.

00:00:32.747 --> 00:00:38.683
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language,
and is used to add content to our webpage.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.488
For example, I can write this
bit of HTML to add a button.

00:00:43.992 --> 00:00:48.712
I can also add other types of content,
like images and text, and if I write more

00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:51.674
HTML, it will add more content to my page.

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:54.483
But you'll notice that our
page doesn't look very good.

00:00:54.986 --> 00:00:56.316
That's where CSS comes in.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:01.147
CSS stands for Cascading Style
Sheets, and based on the name,

00:01:01.162 --> 00:01:03.186
it's used to style our webpage.

00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:07.027
So it's used to make our webpage look
good by letting us adjust colors,

00:01:07.151 --> 00:01:09.602
sizes, spacing, things like that.

00:01:10.275 --> 00:01:12.062
Let's add some CSS to our page.

00:01:13.562 --> 00:01:15.967
So now that looks much better,
but we're not done here.

00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:20.241
Let's say that I want to filter these
products and only show one brand of

00:01:20.241 --> 00:01:24.126
shoes, so I click this checkbox, but
you'll notice that nothing happens,

00:01:24.663 --> 00:01:26.222
and this is where JavaScript comes in.

00:01:27.007 --> 00:01:31.969
HTML and CSS only display things
on our webpage, but JavaScript is

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:33.889
what makes our webpage interactive.

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:38.074
So for example, when we click
something and type something, a

00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:41.358
bit of JavaScript code will update
the page based on what we did.

00:01:42.775 --> 00:01:45.554
The most important feature of
JavaScript is something called the

00:01:45.573 --> 00:01:48.019
Document Object Model, or the DOM.

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:51.204
The DOM allows JavaScript
to change the webpage.

00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:55.916
For example, this bit of JavaScript
code uses the DOM to change the

00:01:55.916 --> 00:02:00.227
text in the button, and this is how
JavaScript makes pages interactive.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.334
However, using the DOM directly
like this is really repetitive

00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:08.168
and hard to manage, and that's
why we use a JavaScript framework.

00:02:09.107 --> 00:02:13.312
JavaScript frameworks give us a much
nicer way to create our webpage, and they

00:02:13.313 --> 00:02:17.248
take care of updating the page for us,
so we never use the DOM directly anymore.

00:02:17.882 --> 00:02:20.205
The most popular ones are React.js,

00:02:20.347 --> 00:02:21.873
Angular, and Vue.js.

00:02:22.951 --> 00:02:25.437
Now let's take a step back
and look at JavaScript again.

00:02:26.154 --> 00:02:29.024
JavaScript as a language is
missing a lot of features that

00:02:29.064 --> 00:02:30.605
other programming languages have.

00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:34.372
One of these features is being able
to split up our code into different

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:36.277
files and to organize our code.

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:39.504
To solve this, we have to use
something called a bundler.

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:43.176
The most popular one you've
probably heard of is called Webpack.

00:02:43.694 --> 00:02:48.108
Webpack lets us split up our JavaScript
code into many different files, and

00:02:48.109 --> 00:02:51.675
once we're ready to put it on our
website, Webpack will combine all

00:02:51.675 --> 00:02:55.786
of these files, or bundle all of
these files, into one JavaScript

00:02:55.791 --> 00:02:57.757
file that we can put on our website.

00:02:58.554 --> 00:03:01.200
Another tool that we use
is called a transpiler.

00:03:01.664 --> 00:03:04.826
The most popular one you probably
heard of is called TypeScript.

00:03:05.473 --> 00:03:08.598
A transpiler adds extra
features onto JavaScript.

00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:12.663
It lets us write an enhanced version
of JavaScript, and then once we're

00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:17.620
done, it will transform the enhanced
JavaScript back into normal JavaScript,

00:03:17.956 --> 00:03:21.978
because browsers like Google Chrome
can only understand normal JavaScript.

00:03:22.707 --> 00:03:25.849
So those are the major technologies
in the JavaScript world.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.287
Now we're gonna move over to CSS.

00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:32.583
CSS as a language has the
same problems as JavaScript.

00:03:33.016 --> 00:03:36.859
We can't organize our code into
different files, and CSS is missing a

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:42.132
lot of useful features, so we also use
a bundler and a transpiler for CSS.

00:03:42.652 --> 00:03:46.332
There's a special name for
this called a CSS preprocessor.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:49.777
It's basically the combination
of a bundler and a transpiler.

00:03:50.378 --> 00:03:54.832
The most popular CSS preprocessor
you probably heard of is called Sass.

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:59.709
Sass lets us organize our CSS into
different files, and it lets us write

00:03:59.743 --> 00:04:04.247
enhanced CSS, and then it will bundle
all of those files together into

00:04:04.248 --> 00:04:09.606
one file, and it will transform the
enhanced CSS back into normal CSS.

00:04:09.987 --> 00:04:13.890
So you can think of it sort of like
a bundler and a transpiler combined.

00:04:14.376 --> 00:04:18.370
Now, once we had CSS preprocessors,
developers started to create

00:04:18.697 --> 00:04:22.700
CSS frameworks, the most
popular one being Bootstrap.

00:04:23.578 --> 00:04:27.302
CSS frameworks are basically a whole
bunch of CSS code that someone else

00:04:27.302 --> 00:04:31.979
wrote, and it helps us solve common
problems, so using a framework like

00:04:31.996 --> 00:04:33.885
Bootstrap saves us a lot of time.

00:04:35.204 --> 00:04:37.447
The last thing we're gonna look
at is how does the front end

00:04:37.466 --> 00:04:38.921
communicate with the back end?

00:04:39.537 --> 00:04:42.852
For example, let's say I'm on
Amazon and I want to make an order.

00:04:43.337 --> 00:04:46.748
How does this order get sent
from my computer to Amazon?

00:04:47.427 --> 00:04:51.769
JavaScript has a feature for
this too called XML HTTP Request.

00:04:52.147 --> 00:04:53.451
It lets us send a message.

00:04:53.638 --> 00:04:56.982
In this case, we're gonna send
our Amazon order to a URL,

00:04:57.058 --> 00:04:59.519
like amazon.com/createorder.

00:05:00.014 --> 00:05:04.770
On the other side, Amazon's back end is
listening for these messages sent to this

00:05:04.774 --> 00:05:07.307
URL, and that's how they get our order.

00:05:07.842 --> 00:05:10.794
We're gonna talk more about this
process in our back end video that

00:05:10.794 --> 00:05:12.061
you can find in the description.

00:05:12.676 --> 00:05:16.775
Over the years, we've improved on
XML HTTP Request, and these days

00:05:16.777 --> 00:05:21.219
we use tools like Axios or Fetch
to send messages to the back end.

00:05:22.147 --> 00:05:24.472
All right, so these are all
the technologies we need

00:05:24.472 --> 00:05:25.693
to build the front end.

00:05:26.102 --> 00:05:30.085
You'll notice that it's really
just HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:34.278
The other technologies make CSS
and JavaScript easier to work with.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.852
Let me know in the comments how many
of these technologies you already

00:05:37.852 --> 00:05:39.674
knew, and if anything was new to you.

